    "slug": "over-160-displaced-families-evicted-from-baidoa-camp",
    "summary": "Landowners in Baidoa forcibly evicted more than 160 IDP families from Iraroog camp without notice, leaving them without income or access to basic services.",
    "content": "More than 160 internally displaced families were forcibly evicted from a camp in Baidoa, Somalia, after landowners who had initially invited them to settle there ordered them to leave. The eviction took place without prior notice, forcing families to abandon their belongings and sources of income. The camp, known as Iraroog, had been developed with free water, healthcare, and education services.\n\nThe eviction has left the families in a precarious situation, with no income and limited access to basic needs. Among those affected is Abdirahman Ali Isaaq, who ran a small shop selling goods and vegetables, earning between $3 and $5 a day to support his family of nine. Like many others, he lost his business and assets overnight.\n\nThe incident is part of a broader pattern of land disputes in Baidoa, where displaced families often settle on private land with informal agreements that can later be revoked. The lack of formal tenure or legal protection makes IDPs vulnerable to sudden evictions. Local authorities have not yet commented on the eviction or provided alternative solutions for the affected families.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event highlights the ongoing challenges faced by displaced populations in their homeland. Many diaspora members send remittances to support relatives in IDP camps, and sudden evictions disrupt these lifelines. The loss of schools and health facilities in the camp also means that children miss education and families lack medical care, compounding the hardship.\n\nThe eviction underscores the need for durable solutions for IDPs in Somalia, including secure land rights and access to basic services. Without such measures, families remain at risk of repeated displacement and deepening poverty. The diaspora can play a role by advocating for the rights of IDPs and supporting organizations that provide emergency assistance and legal aid.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/idp-families-kicked-off-baidoa-land-lose-their-assets-income-schools-and-health-centre/",
